<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Felix Manz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1498–<a href="/years/1527">1527</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Felix Manz was a Swiss Anabaptist leader from Zürich and a former follower of Zwingli. With Conrad Grebel and George Blaurock he took part in the first adult baptisms in January 1525. Repeatedly imprisoned for preaching, he was drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limmat">Limmat</a> on 5 January 1527, the first Anabaptist executed by a Protestant authority.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Felix Manz, also written Mantz, was born in Zürich, in the Old Swiss Confederacy, where his father was a canon of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grossmunster">Grossmünster</a> church. Records of his education are scant, but there is evidence that he received a liberal education and had a thorough knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. He became a follower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a> after the reformer came to Zürich in 1519, and when Conrad Grebel joined the circle in 1521 the two men became friends.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Manz and Grebel came to question the mass, the ties between church and state, and the baptism of infants. After the Second Disputation of Zürich in 1523 they grew dissatisfied, believing that Zwingli had compromised his plans for reform with the city council. Several parents in their circle refused to have their children baptised. A public disputation with Zwingli was held on 17 January 1525, and the council declared Zwingli the victor.</p><p>On 18 January the council ordered the brethren to stop arguing and to have their children baptised within eight days. Three days later they gathered at the home of Manz and his mother, where the first baptisms of the Swiss Brethren took place, completing the break with Zwingli and the council. Manz used his languages to translate texts into the speech of the people and worked as an evangelist. He was arrested several times between 1525 and 1527, and was finally taken while preaching with George Blaurock in the Grüningen region and held in the Wellenburg prison in Zürich.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 7 March 1526 the Zürich council had made adult rebaptism punishable by drowning, and Manz was the first to die under that edict. Zwingli and the council accused him of obstinately refusing to recede from his error. As he was led from the Wellenburg to a boat he preached to the crowd, while his mother and brother urged him to stand firm. His hands were bound behind his knees with a pole between them. His property was confiscated and he was buried in the St. Jakobs cemetery.</p><p>Manz left an eighteen-stanza hymn of praise, preserved in the Ausbund, a 16th-century hymn book still used by the Amish, and was apparently the author of the Protestation und Schutzschrift, a defence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anabaptism">Anabaptism</a> presented to the Zürich council. The Amish, Baptist, Mennonite and Bruderhof churches are all influenced in varying degrees by him and the other Anabaptists of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Felix Manz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zürich was also the birthplace of the Anabaptist movement, whose first adult baptisms took place there in 1525 and whose leaders were persecuted; <b>Felix Manz</b> was drowned in the Limmat in 1527.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Conrad Grebel, <b>Felix Manz</b> and George Blaurock baptised one another as believing adults in Zurich.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Zurich, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. Died in Zurich, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.37,8.54&amp;year=1516" aria-label="Zurich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4750 502 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Felix Manz"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5200 772h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5200 772h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.37,8.54&amp;year=1516">What was here in 1516? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
